\chapter{Applications to Astrophysical Simulation}

\section{Introduction}
The Unified Kinematic–Geometric Simulation Engine (UKG-SE) is designed not only as a 
theoretical and computational framework but as a practical tool for simulating 
astrophysical systems under extreme gravitational and kinematic conditions. This 
chapter presents the primary applications of UKG-SE v2.0, demonstrating its ability 
to model galactic clusters, black hole mergers, momentum flux fields, and encrypted 
observational datasets.

\section{Astrophysical Dataset Integration}

\subsection{Encrypted Ingestion Pipeline}
UKG-SE integrates real astrophysical datasets through a quantum-resistant encrypted 
pipeline using:
\begin{itemize}
    \item AES-256-GCM,
    \item SHA-3 hashing,
    \item TLS 1.3 secure transport.
\end{itemize}

Let $\mathcal{D}_{\text{raw}}$ denote raw observational data. The encrypted dataset is:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_{\text{enc}} = \text{AES256GCM}(\mathcal{D}_{\text{raw}}).
\end{equation}

After decryption:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_{\text{norm}} = \text{Normalize}(\mathcal{D}_{\text{enc}}),
\end{equation}
which feeds directly into the solver modules.

